    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d337-a2">

                <pb facs="0799" n="715" xml:id="pg715"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 18.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Oglesby to Mr.
                            Linn.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Eagle Pass, Tex.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1886-07-27"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 27,
                        1886</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: On the morning of the 26th a warrant
                    for the arrest and delivery of Francisco Arresuris was sent me from Piedras
                    Negras, Mexico; upon receipt of same, I had him arrested and obtained an order
                    from the county judgo to deliver him, and did so, taking a receipt for said
                    party. This morning I was told that he was an American citizen; have examined
                    the records here of the county and district clerk’s office, and find that the
                    said party declared his intentions to become a citizen in 1873. This is about
                    all I know about the matter. As I have been accustomed in like manner heretofore
                    to deliver up parties under same circumstances, thought it was coming under the
                    province of the law.</p>
                <p>Most respectfully, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>T. L. OGLESBY,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Sheriff, Maverick County,
                            Texas.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
